Step 1: Sensitivity-Based Method (SBM) — Equity Risk
What is Delta Sensitivity for Equity?
For a linear cash equity position, the delta sensitivity is simply the market value of the position.

Delta Sensitivity:

S(k) = Delta(k) × Market Value
For a cash equity position:

Delta(k) = 1
Therefore:

S(k) = Market Value
This means that a ₹1 change in the stock price results in approximately a ₹1 change in the position value.

FRTB SA Equity Buckets
Under the FRTB Standardised Approach (SA), equities are assigned to buckets based on market capitalization, sector, and geography. Each bucket is associated with a prescribed risk weight and correlation parameter. The official framework has 13 buckets total (1–4: large-cap EM, 5–8: large-cap DM, 9: small-cap EM, 10: small-cap DM, 11: other sector, 12–13: index). A representative subset is shown below for illustration:

Bucket	Description	Risk Weight (RW)	Intra-bucket Correlation
1	Large-cap Emerging Market — Consumer, Transport	55%	15%
2	Large-cap Emerging Market — Telecom, Industrials	60%	15%
4	Large-cap Emerging Market — Financials, Real Estate, Tech	55%	15%
7	Large-cap Developed Market — Basic Materials, Energy	35%	15%
8	Large-cap Developed Market — Financials, Real Estate, Tech	45%	15%
9	Small-cap Emerging Market	70%	7.5%
10	Small-cap Developed Market	50%	7.5%
11	Other sector	70%	15%
Correction note: An earlier version of this table mislabeled Bucket 8 as "Emerging Market — Energy, Mining." Buckets 5–8 are all Developed Market — Bucket 8 is correctly "Large-cap DM — Financials, Real Estate, Technology." Buckets 3, 5, 6 (other EM/DM sector splits) are omitted here for brevity; they follow the same large-cap EM/DM sectoral pattern.

BOB classification: Bank of Baroda is an Emerging Market, large-cap financial institution — so it most precisely belongs in Bucket 4 (EM Financials/Real Estate/Tech, RW 55%), not Bucket 8 (which is DM). The 55% risk weight used in this notebook is correct for Bucket 4; only the earlier bucket label was wrong.

Verify before relying on this for production/interview use: exact risk weight and correlation values shown here should be cross-checked against the current official BCBS/BIS MAR21 table, since Basel 3.1 / CRR3 calibration updates have adjusted some bucket values over time.

Delta Capital Charge
The weighted sensitivity is obtained by multiplying the sensitivity by the prescribed risk weight.

Weighted Sensitivity:

WS(k) = RW × S(k)
For a single equity position, the delta capital charge simplifies to:

K_Equity_Delta = RW × |S(k)|
where:

S(k) = Equity delta sensitivity
RW = Prescribed FRTB risk weight
Vega Risk
For option positions, FRTB separately captures volatility risk through vega sensitivities.

A simplified vega sensitivity is:

VR(k) = Vega(k) × Volatility Shock
where:

Vega(k) = Black–Scholes vega of the option position
Volatility Shock = Assumed change in implied volatility
The corresponding vega capital charge is:

K_Equity_Vega = RW_Vega × |VR(k)|
For illustration, this notebook assumes:

Volatility Shock = 5%
Simplification note: This notebook reuses the Delta risk weight as the Vega risk weight for simplicity. In practice, Basel prescribes a separate vega risk weight table per risk class (not identical to delta RW) — this is a pedagogical simplification, not the production Basel treatment.

Key Takeaway
The Sensitivity-Based Method (SBM) converts market exposures into risk-weighted sensitivities. These weighted sensitivities are then aggregated using prescribed FRTB correlation assumptions to determine the final Standardised Approach capital requirement.

In practice:

Market Exposure
      ↓
Sensitivity
      ↓
Risk Weight
      ↓
Weighted Sensitivity
      ↓
Correlation Aggregation
      ↓
Capital Requirement
Note on correlation aggregation: This notebook holds a single position per risk class, so there is no second position within the same bucket to correlate against — the High/Medium/Low correlation scenario mechanism (ρ scaled by 1.25× / 1.0× / max(2ρ−1, 0.75ρ)) becomes relevant only once a bucket holds two or more positions. It is therefore not shown numerically here, but is an important extension point.


