Key Findings
1. SA Floor is binding

Final Capital=max(₹28,70,716, ₹67,80,607)=₹67,80,607

SA Floor exceeds IMA Capital — the bank must hold SA-based capital, not its own internal model estimate. This is precisely the output floor mechanism introduced under Basel III (finalisation)/Basel IV.

2. Equity dominates SA capital

Equity (Delta+Vega, ₹55.4L) represents ~59% of total SA capital (₹93.5L). Expected for an equity-heavy book — equity carries the highest risk weight among all risk classes in this portfolio.

3. DRC is material

At ₹15L, DRC is a large component, broadly comparable in size to the combined FX and Commodity charges (₹16.25L). This highlights why DRC exists as a separate charge: jump-to-default risk is not captured by SBM sensitivities.

4. GIRR is meaningfully material — not negligible

The corrected GIRR delta charge (₹6.9L) is smaller than Equity's charge, which is directionally expected — rates are typically less volatile than equity prices, so GIRR's prescribed risk weight (1.15%, applied to a duration-scaled sensitivity) is proportionally lower than Equity's 55% risk weight. But at ~7.4% of total SBM, it is a genuine, material contributor — not the near-zero figure the uncorrected calculation implied. A larger or longer-duration fixed income book would scale this up further.

5. RRAO is zero for the actual portfolio

The real hypothetical book holds no options or exotic instruments — all four positions (Equity, FX, Commodity, G-Sec) are linear. RRAO is therefore ₹0 in the SA total. A separate illustrative RRAO (~₹5,000) was computed in Step 6 purely to demonstrate the mechanic on a hypothetical option, but is intentionally excluded from this final aggregation.

Why is SA > IMA for this portfolio?
Feature	IMA	SA
Diversification	Captures historical correlations	No cross-risk-class netting
Risk Measure	ES — tail-sensitive	Fixed RW — conservative
Stress	Integrated in stressed ES	Separate DRC component
Flexibility	Bank's own model	Regulator prescribed
IMA capital is lower because it uses actual historical correlations and captures diversification across risk classes. SA is conservative by design — fixed risk weights, no diversification benefit.

The 72.5% floor ensures banks cannot fully exploit this gap — internal models can reduce capital, but only down to 72.5% of SA.

Limitations of this Illustration
Curvature risk not implemented numerically — requires full re-pricing under stressed scenarios (non-linear SBM component); worked separately in discussion.
Correlation scenarios (High/Medium/Low) not shown numerically — each risk class here holds only a single position, so there is no second position within a bucket to correlate against. This becomes material once a bucket holds two or more positions (see Step 1 markdown note).
Multi-tenor GIRR simplified to single 10Y tenor — real implementation aggregates across full yield curve.
Vega calculated for equity only — real book includes FX vol surface and commodity vol surface.
FX risk weight uses the flat 15%; the possible 10.61% "specified pair" treatment for USD/INR has not been confirmed (see Step 2 code comment).
Commodity and GIRR risk weights (35%, 1.15%, etc.) should be verified against the current official BCBS table before production/interview use.
Sovereign DRC = 0% is a simplification; not independently re-confirmed against the current official table for all jurisdictions.
Position sizes are hypothetical — actual bank positions are proprietary and significantly larger.
